 26, 2026 by phlax\n\nHigh\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via Unsafe Attribute Construction and `info` Field HTML Insertion · Advisory · opnsense/core · GitHub (https://github.com/opnsense/core/security/advisories/GHSA-75fw-j788-j4hj)\n【turn776137search5】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; # Stored XSS via Unsafe Attribute Construction and `info` Field HTML Insertion ... path requires a matching live security association and was not separately ... Suricata Metadata Property Stored XSS in IDS Policy Editor\n\n# Stored XSS via Unsafe Attribute Construction and `info` Field HTML Insertion\n\nModerate\n\nfichtner published GHSA-75fw-j788-j4hj Aug 12, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nopnsense/core\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 26.7.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n26.7.2\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\nFour stored cross-site scripting sink variants appear to remain outside the\n`d0ffce2` fix for GHSA-jm2f-ch62-rpxr. They are caused by unsafe HTML attribute\nconstruction in OPNsense WebGUI pages, where stored or cached values are\ninterpolated into HTML strings without attribute-context escaping.\n\nDynamic browser execution was confirmed for:\n\n  1. Firewall Alias category tooltip attribute injection\n  2. IPsec SPD description tooltip attribute injection\n  3. Traffic Shaper relation description tooltip attribute injection in Firewall Rules\n  4. Suricata metadata property attribute injection in IDS Policy Editor\n\nThe duplicate IPsec SAD renderer is static-equivalent to the confirmed SPD sink\nbut was not separately confirmed with a live browser alert.\n\nTested affected version: OPNsense `26.1.11_5`.\n\n## Relationship to GHSA-jm2f-ch62-rpxr / `d0ffce2`\n\nThe following previously reported `info` field issues appear to be covered by\n`d0ffce2` and are not included as active missing items in this follow-up:\n\n  * Suricata rule `msg` metadata XSS, through the generic `opnsense.js` change\nthat removed `htmlDecode()` before insertion into `span` fields.\n  * Auth Group/User `scope` DOM XSS, through the same generic form mapper change.\n\nThe relevant `d0ffce2` change is:\n    \n    - targetNode.append(htmlDecode(node[keypart]));\n    + targetNode.append(node[keypart]);\n\nThis follow-up focuses on the remaining unsafe attribute-construction sinks\nthat do not appear to be covered by `d0ffce2`.\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Firewall Alias Category Tooltip Attribute Injection\n\nSuggested severity: Medium when a referenced category already exists — the\nwriter privilege (`page-firewall-categories`) and victim privilege\n(`page-firewall-aliases`) are separate. GHSA-2xrm-p255-p43h fixed the same\na controlled kernel SPD entry with the test child's `reqid` supplied the row;\nin a normal deployment, an established IPsec policy supplies it. Open\n`/ui/ipsec/spd` and hover the information icon. Generated DOM:\n    \n    <span class=\"fa fa-fw fa-info-circle\"\n          title=\"IPSEC714A'B\"\n          onmouseenter=\"alert(714301)\"\n          data-x=\"\"\n          data-toggle=\"tooltip\"></span>\n\nSAD status: `sad.volt` contains the same vulnerable formatter, but the SAD\npath requires a matching live security association and was not separately\nconfirmed with a browser alert. It should be described as static-equivalent\nuntil that runtime path is tested.\n\n\n### 3. Traffic Shaper Relation Description Attribute Injection in Firewall Rules\n\nSuggested severity: Medium when a referenced shaper object already exists —\nthe Traffic Shaper writer privilege (`page-firewall-trafficshaper`) and Firewall\nRules privilege (`page-filter-api`) are separate. This is a distinct sink from\nGHSA-m4m3-v627-wgc2, which fixed the legacy PHP\n### 4. Suricata Metadata Property Stored XSS in IDS Policy Editor\n\nSuggested severity: Medium under the same threat model as the existing\nSuricata ruleset-metadata XSS report — exploitation requires control or\ncompromise of an enabled ruleset source and administrator interaction. This\nshould be appended to that report as an additional affected renderer.\n\nA controlled or compromised enabled Suricata ruleset can define arbitrary\nmetadata property names and values. OPNsense stores them in the rule metadata\ncache and returns them as `PolicyContentField` options. On `/ui/ids/policy`,\nthe property name is concatenated into a single-quoted `<select id='...'>`\nstring, while the full option key and display value are also concatenated into\nraw `<option>` strings. A single quote in the property name creates an event\nhandler attribute. The page then programmatically triggers `change` on the\ngenerated select, executing the handler.\n\nThe IDS change in `d0ffce2` appears limited to `reference_html` sanitization in\n`getRuleInfoAction()`. ## Root Cause Analysis\n\nThese findings share a concrete root cause at the final rendering boundary:\nuntrusted values are interpolated into HTML strings.\n\nThe affected Volt renderers concatenate stored values into quoted attributes or\noption markup. Once JSON has been parsed, quotes are ordinary characters in the\nJavaScript string and must be encoded for the destination HTML context or\nassigned through DOM APIs.\n\nTransport-level encoding is not sufficient for these remaining issues. Once a\nJSON string has been parsed by JavaScript, characters such as quotes are again\nordinary string content. They must be escaped for the final HTML attribute\ncontext or assigned safely through DOM APIs.\n\n## Classification Summary\n\n#  | Finding  | Suggested classification  | Source actor  | Victim / trigger  | Required relationship  | Evidence status\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n1  | Alias Category XSS  | Medium  | `page-firewall-categories` user  | `page-firewall-aliases` user hovers category dot  | Category already assigned to an alias  | Dynamic execution confirmed; delegated roles not separately exercised\n2  | IPsec description XSS  | Medium  | `page-vpn-ipsec-connections` user  | `page-status-ipsec-spd` user hovers info icon  | Matching runtime SPD row  | SPD dynamic confirmed; SAD static-equivalent\n3  | Traffic Shaper Relation XSS  | Medium  | `page-firewall-trafficshaper` user  | `page-filter-api` user hovers advanced icon  | Rule already references pipe or queue  | Dynamic execution confirmed; delegated roles not separately exercised\n4  | Suricata metadata XSS  | Medium, consistent with parent report  | Controlled or compromised enabled ruleset  | Administrator opens IDS policy editor  | Trusted ruleset path  | Dynamic confirmed\n\n## Remediation\n\n### Primary fix: output encoding at the rendering sink\n\nFor the attribute-injection sinks above, build HTML elements with DOM APIs and\nassign attribute values through `.attr()` or `.prop()`, or use `textContent`\nwhere text-only output is intended. Never use string concatenation to build HTML\nattributes from untrusted values.\n\nFor the IDS Policy editor, create both `<select>` and `<option>` elements with\nDOM APIs and assign IDs, values, and labels separately.\n\n### Defense-in-depth validation\n\n  * Constrain identifiers and enumerated fields to their supported grammar.\nIn particular, validate Suricata metadata keys and values.\n  * Do not treat server-side or transport-level entity encoding as a substitute\nfor output handling at the final DOM sink.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.2\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nHigh\n\nUser interaction\n\nRequired\n\nScope\n\nUnchanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nHigh\n\nIntegrity\n\nLow\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * lujiefsi Reporter\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXSS via text/html content files · Advisory · gohugoio/hugo · GitHub (https://github.com/gohugoio/hugo/security/advisories/GHSA-c54g-xjwj-8g82)\n【turn776137search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; # XSS via text/html content files ... Mitigation. v0.162.0 introduces a `security.allowContent` whitelist with `text/html` denied by default.\n\n# XSS via text/html content files\n\nModerate\n\nbep published GHSA-c54g-xjwj-8g82 May 28, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/gohugoio/hugo (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< v0.162.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\nv0.162.0.\n\n## Description\n\nCommit: e41a06447d — Disallow HTML content by default\nAffected versions: all Hugo versions prior to v0.162.0.\nFixed in: v0.162.0.\nSeverity: Low to Medium, depending on threat model. Not an issue if you fully trust every file under `/content` and every content adapter you load.\n\nDescription. Hugo accepts content files in several markup formats. Files mapped to the `text/html` media type (typically `.html` files under `/content`, or pages produced by a content adapter that sets `content.mediaType = \"text/html\"`) had their body emitted verbatim into the rendered page. A site that ingests HTML content from an untrusted source — for example, a CMS-backed editor, a content adapter pulling from an external API, or an automated import pipeline — could therefore be served stored cross-site scripting.\n\nMitigation. v0.162.0 introduces a `security.allowContent` whitelist with `text/html` denied by default. Sites that intentionally author HTML content can opt back in:\n    \n    [security]\n    allowContent = ['.*']\n\nThis only affects pages whose source file (or content adapter output) declares an HTML media type; Markdown, AsciiDoc, Org, Pandoc and reStructuredText content is unaffected.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-50133\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n\n### Credits\n\n  * jmooring Analyst\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPotential Referer-Based Reflected HTML Injection / XSS in Tag Update Page · Advisory · mantisbt/mantisbt · GitHub (https://github.com/mantisbt/mantisbt/security/advisories/GHSA-6jh4-47v2-4g37)\n【turn776137search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Potential Referer-Based Reflected HTML Injection / XSS in Tag Update Page ... These differ from security-enhancing techniques/technologies (ref Attack Complexity) as the primary purpose of these conditions is not to explicitly mitigate attacks, but rather, emerge naturally as a consequence of the deployment and execution of the vulnerable system.\n\n# Potential Referer-Based Reflected HTML Injection / XSS in Tag Update Page\n\nModerate\n\ndregad published GHSA-6jh4-47v2-4g37 May 9, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nmantisbt/mantisbt (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 2.28.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2.28.2\n\n## Description\n\nImproper escaping of the redirection page (retrieved from the request's Referer header) allows an attacker to inject HTML.\n\nWhile this is generally not directly actionable as modern browsers will URL-encode special characters, on some specific server configurations this could poison the cache, leading to cross-site scripting.\n\n### Impact\n\nCross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n### Patches\n\n  * `b1ebc57`\n\n### Workarounds\n\nNone\n\n### References\n\n  * https://mantisbt.org/bugs/view.php?id=37017\n\n### Credits\n\nThanks to siunam (Tang Cheuk Hei) for discovering and responsibly reporting the issue.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.3\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS) · Advisory · symfony/symfony · GitHub (https://github.com/symfony/symfony/security/advisories/GHSA-hhg7-c65m-h7ff)\n【turn776137search8】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last week; # HtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS)\n\n# HtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS)\n\nLow\n\nnicolas-grekas published GHSA-hhg7-c65m-h7ff May 20, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nsymfony/html-sanitizer (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>=6.1, <6.4.40\n\n>=7, <7.4.12\n\n>=8, <8.0.12\n\n## Patched versions\n\n6.4.40\n\n7.4.12\n\n8.0.12\n\nsymfony/symfony (Composer)\n\n>=6.1, <6.4.40\n\n>=7, <7.4.12\n\n>=8, <8.0.12\n\n6.4.40\n\n7.4.12\n\n8.0.12\n\n## Description\n\n### Description\n\n`symfony/html-sanitizer` lets applications sanitise untrusted HTML. `UrlAttributeSanitizer` is the visitor responsible for validating URL-valued attributes and stripping dangerous schemes from them; it runs on every element regardless of configuration. Whether an attribute is kept is decided by the element/attribute allow-list; validating the scheme of a URL attribute is solely `UrlAttributeSanitizer`'s responsibility.\n\n`UrlAttributeSanitizer::getSupportedAttributes()` returned only `['src', 'href', 'lowsrc', 'background', 'ping']`. The HTML URL-valued attributes `action` (`<form>`), `formaction` (`<button>`, `<input type=image>`), `poster` (`<video>`) and `cite` (`<blockquote>`, `<q>`, `<del>`, `<ins>`) were missing from that list, so `DomVisitor` never invoked scheme validation for them. As a result, when a configuration admits one of those attributes, a `javascript:` URI in it survived sanitisation.\n\n### Conditions for exploitation\n\n`allowSafeElements()` is not affected: `<form>` and the `formaction` attribute are both flagged unsafe in `W3CReference`, and `allowElement('form')` resets the element's attribute list. Reaching the vulnerable attributes requires a deliberately permissive configuration, for example:\n\n  * `<form>` + `action`: `allowElement('form', '*')`, `allowElement('form', ['action', …])`, `allowElement('form')->allowAttribute('action', 'form')`, or the `allowStaticElements()` preset (whose docblock already warns the output \"may still contain other dangerous behaviors\");\n  * `<button>` / `<input type=image>` + `formaction`: `allowElement(…, '*')`, `allowAttribute('formaction', …)`, or `allowStaticElements()`;\n  * `<blockquote>` / `<q>` / `<del>` / `<ins>` + `cite`, or `<video>` + `poster`: similarly via `'*'`, `allowAttribute()`, or `allowStaticElements()`.\n\nFor the `action` / `formaction` cases the victim must additionally submit the form or click the button.\n\n### Resolution\n\n`UrlAttributeSanitizer` now also handles `action`, `formaction`, `cite` and `poster`. `action` / `formaction` / `cite` are validated against the link schemes (like `<a href>`, so `javascript:` is rejected and `data:` is dropped too); `poster` is validated against the media schemes (so `data:` images keep working). The behaviour of `<a href>` and `<img src>` is unchanged.\n\nOne behaviour change to be aware of: a relative `action=\"/submit\"` on an allowed `<form>` is now dropped by default (the same as `<a href>` / `<img src>` today); `->allowRelativeLinks()` re-enables it.\n\nThe patch for this issue is available here for branch 6.4.\n\n### Credits\n\nWe would like to thank Himanshu Anand and Rémi Pelloux for reporting the issue and Nicolas Grekas for providing the fix.\n\n### Severity\n\nLow\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-45753\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\nWeakness CWE-184\n\n#### Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs\n\nThe product implements a protection mechanism that relies on a list of inputs (or properties of inputs) that are not allowed by policy or otherwise require other action to neutralize before additional processing takes place, but the list is incomplete. Learn more on MITRE.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via file upload of HTML-renderable file types · Advisory · parse-community/parse-server · GitHub (https://github.com/parse-community/parse-server/security/advisories/GHSA-v5hf-f4c3-m5rv)\n【turn776137search9】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; # Stored XSS via file upload of HTML-renderable file types ... These differ from security-enhancing techniques/technologies (ref Attack Complexity) as the primary purpose of these conditions is not to explicitly mitigate attacks, but rather, emerge naturally as a consequence of the deployment and execution of the vulnerable system.\n\n# Stored XSS via file upload of HTML-renderable file types\n\nModerate\n\nmtrezza published GHSA-v5hf-f4c3-m5rv Mar 10, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nparse-server (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 9.0.0 < 9.6.0-alpha.4\n\n< 8.6.30\n\n## Patched versions\n\n9.6.0-alpha.4\n\n8.6.30\n\n## Description\n\n### Impact\n\nAn attacker can upload a file with a file extension or content type that is not blocked by the default configuration of the Parse Server `fileUpload.fileExtensions` option. The file can contain malicious code, for example JavaScript in an SVG or XHTML file. When the file is accessed via its URL, the browser renders the file and executes the malicious code in the context of the Parse Server domain. This is a stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability that can be exploited to steal session tokens, redirect users, or perform actions on behalf of other users.\n\nAffected file extensions and content types include `.svgz`, `.xht`, `.xml`, `.xsl`, `.xslt`, and content types `application/xhtml+xml` and `application/xslt+xml` for extensionless uploads. Integrity refers to the trustworthiness and veracity of information. Integrity of the SUBSEQUENT SYSTEM is impacted when an attacker makes unauthorized modification of system data. Integrity is also impacted when a system user can repudiate critical actions taken in the context of the system (e.g. due to insufficient logging).\n\nAvailability: This metric measures the impact to the availability of the SUBSEQUENT SYSTEM resulting from a successfully exploited vulnerability. While the Confidentiality and Integrity impact metrics apply to the loss of confidentiality or integrity of data (e.g., information, files) used by the system, this metric refers to the loss of availability of the impacted system itself, such as a networked service (e.g., web, database, email). Since availability refers to the accessibility of information resources, attacks that consume network bandwidth, processor cycles, or disk space all impact the availability of a system.\n\nCVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:A/VC:N/VI:N/VA:N/SC:H/SI:H/SA:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-31868\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * offset Reporter\n  * mtrezza Coordinator\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXSS via Binary Data Inline HTML Rendering · Advisory · n8n-io/n8n · GitHub (https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-qfc3-hm4j-7q77)\n【turn776137search10】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # XSS via Binary Data Inline HTML Rendering ... The `/rest/binary-data` endpoint served such responses inline on the n8n origin without `Content-Disposition` or `Content-Security-Policy` headers, allowing the HTML to render in the browser with full same-origin JavaScript access. ... n8n has adopted CVSS 4.0 as primary score for all security advisories.\n\n# XSS via Binary Data Inline HTML Rendering\n\nModerate\n\nJubke published GHSA-qfc3-hm4j-7q77 Mar 25, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nn8n (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 1.123.27\n\n< 2.14.1\n\n< 2.13.3\n\n## Patched versions\n\n>= 1.123.27\n\n>= 2.14.1\n\n>= 2.13.3\n\n## Description\n\n## Impact\n\nAn authenticated user with permission to create or modify workflows could craft a workflow that produces an HTML binary data object without a filename. The `/rest/binary-data` endpoint served such responses inline on the n8n origin without `Content-Disposition` or `Content-Security-Policy` headers, allowing the HTML to render in the browser with full same-origin JavaScript access.\n\nBy sending the resulting URL to a higher-privileged user, an attacker could execute JavaScript in the victim's authenticated session, enabling exfiltration of workflows and credentials, modification of workflows, or privilege escalation to admin.\n\n## Patches\n\nThe issue has been fixed in n8n versions 1.123.27, 2.13.3, and 2.14.1. Users should upgrade to one of these versions or later to remediate the vulnerability.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, administrators should consider the following temporary mitigations:\n\n  * Limit workflow creation and editing permissions to fully trusted users only.\n  * Restrict network access to the n8n instance to prevent untrusted users from accessing binary data URLs.\n\nThese workarounds do not fully remediate the risk and should only be used as short-term mitigation measures.\n\nn8n has adopted CVSS 4.0 as primary score for all security advisories. CVSS 3.1 vector strings are provided for backwards compatibility.\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:L\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n6.3\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n#### CVSS v4 base metrics\n\n##### Exploitability Metrics\n\nAttack Vector Network\n\nAttack Complexity Low\n\nAttack Requirements None\n\nPrivileges Required Low\n\nUser interaction Active\n\n##### Vulnerable System Impact Metrics\n\nConfidentiality Low\n\nIntegrity Low\n\nAvailability None\n\n##### Subsequent System Impact Metrics\n\nConfidentiality High\n\nIntegrity High\n\nAvailability High\n\nCVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:A/VC:L/VI:L/VA:N/SC:H/SI:H/SA:H\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-33749\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * simonkoeck Reporter\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDOM-Based XSS via Unsandboxed iframe srcdoc in HTML Preview · Advisory · n8n-io/n8n · GitHub (https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-p3rg-hrf9-w9gj)\n【turn776137search11】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; # DOM-Based XSS via Unsandboxed iframe srcdoc in HTML Preview ... These differ from security-enhancing techniques/technologies (ref Attack Complexity) as the primary purpose of these conditions is not to explicitly mitigate attacks, but rather, emerge naturally as a consequence of the deployment and execution of the vulnerable system.\n\n# DOM-Based XSS via Unsandboxed iframe srcdoc in HTML Preview\n\nHigh\n\nJubke published GHSA-p3rg-hrf9-w9gj Jul 8, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nn8n (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 1.123.64\n\n< 2.30.1\n\n< 2.29.8\n\n## Patched versions\n\n>= 1.123.64\n\n>= 2.30.1\n\n>= 2.29.8\n\n## Description\n\n## Impact\n\nThe HTML preview renders execution output into an `iframe srcdoc` without `sandbox`, so a sanitizer bypass lets injected script run same-origin as the editor. When a victim opens the preview, it can call authenticated APIs with their session. An account with `global:member` privileges can exploit it.\n\n## Patches\n\nThe issue has been fixed in n8n versions 1.123.64, 2.29.8, and 2.30.1. Users should upgrade to one of these versions or later to remediate the vulnerability.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, administrators should consider the following temporary mitigations:\n\n  * Restrict n8n instance access to fully trusted users only.\n  * Set the `N8N_CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY` environment variable to a policy that blocks inline scripts.\n"}
